This says the system you got had external disks that it booted from.  However,
you didn't get the disks.

>Now I try:

>>>> BOOT DKB100

># passes INIT seq above

>DEC OSF/1 V3.2 (Rev. 214);Sat Jul 6.....1996

This is probably an old installation.  That it comes up as far as it
does says the root partition is fine.

>/sbin/ufs_fsck -p
>/dev/rrz1a: 1585 files, 45552 used, 17679 free (71 frags, 2201 blocks, 0.1% frag
>mentation
>/dev/rrz3c: /dev/rrz3c: BAD SUPER BLACK: MAGIC NUMBER WRONG
>/dev/rrz3c: UNEXPECTED INCONSISTENCY; RUN fsck MANUALLY
Once upon a time, the system knew rz3 had a UFS.  Apparently the disk has been
reused.

>/dev/rrz1g: File system unmounted cleanly - fsck needed
That should be the /usr partition.  That means you have a complete
installation, but are missing what were probably the customer's disks.
They probably moved to the upgraded machine.

>Can't open /dev/rzz9b: No such device or address
rrz9 would be a external disk, ditto rz11 and rz12.

>/dev/rrz2c: /dev/rrz2c: BAD SUPER BLOCK: MAGIC NUMBER WRONG

>/dev/rrz2c: UNEXPECTED INCONSISTENCY; RUN fsck MANUALLY.
>... /dev/rzz12a, rzz9h, rrz12b, rrz12h: No such device or address .. as above
>THE FOLLOWING FILE SYSTEMS HAD AN UNEXPECTED INCONSISTENCY:
>	/dev/rrz3c (/irm), /dev/rrz2c (/u02)

Definitely user FEes.

>Automatic reboot failed. Run fsck manually.
>Repair filesystems(s), then reboot (/sbin/reboot).

>INIT: SINGLE-USER MODE
>-: stty: not found
>#

>an examination of /etc/fstab reveals several ufs 
>filesystems to mount + swap1, swap2, swap3

Edit fstab to remove the dead FSes.  You'll need to do
"mount -u /" (I think that's the right incantation) to mount root
read-write before editing.

>It seems apparent that if I am going to get anything
>our of this machine (other than parts is parts) I
>must do something to recover the file systems on
>/dev/rrz3c & /dev/rrz2c

Nope.  Customer data.  There's a decent chance the past owner zeroed out
the partition instead of taking the disks out and destroying them.

Do "disklabel /dev/rrz2c" (and 3c) to see what FSes should be on them.

It seems to me that at the very worst, you can boot from rz1 and make
rz2 and rz3 new FSes.

Support for those systems is "officially" gone in V5.1, I think,  the code
to support them is really gon in V5.1A.  Or something like that.

	-Ric Werme
